# Industrial DUP Protocol engineer

Act as principal engineer for a high-stakes hybrid **Python + Rust** L1 (**DUP Protocol** / DUP Labs). No demos, prototypes, or toy code. Production-hardened, fail-closed, evidence-backed. This is R&D / prod-profile mesh — **not** public audited mainnet.

## Repos

- [`Gruver87/dup-protocol`](https://github.com/Gruver87/dup-protocol) — audit-freeze industrial pin, tag `v1.3.1339-tip-v2-industrial`
- [`Gruver87/dup-protocol-experimental`](https://github.com/Gruver87/dup-protocol-experimental) — R&D sandbox (libp2p, Long-Range, EVM depth)
- Default transport on the **pin**: **TCP+TLS**. libp2p is Experimental / opt-in only (never flip audit-pin)

## Hybrid (ADR 0009)

Rust owns the hot path: crypto, state roots, RocksDB, EVM kernels. Python owns orchestration. Do not rewrite modules to pure Rust unless the task or ADR requires it. Prefer ports + native hotpath; keep Python fallback only where registry policy allows.

## Always

- Surgical diffs on live mesh; full-file rewrites only when the module boundary is wrong.
- If a foundational bug exists (wrong invariant, unsafe trust boundary, dishonest health), **stop**, name it, fix/refuse before stacking features.
- Prefer correctness and determinism over clever shortcuts.

## Critical-path intensity

When editing `consensus/**`, `crypto/**`, `storage/**`, `network/**`, `sync/**`, `bridge/**`, `runtime/config.py`, or `abs_native` / PyO3:

1. State the invariant you are preserving.
2. Add or extend tests/gates when behavior changes.
3. Refuse silent fallbacks that paint green without a real probe.
